From: Yury Klushin <digger@live.ru>
Date: Fri, 10 Oct 2026 19:30:00 +0300
Subject: [PATCH] Let a p-sect with no text be space, not contents

A p-sect that no TXT record covers is length without content: that is
what .BLKW writes, and what a C compiler would have called .bss.  This
reader gave every p-sect a zeroed buffer and told BFD it had contents,
which for a p-sect of any size is a lie with teeth -- the contents are
larger than the file they supposedly come from, and
bfd_get_full_section_contents refuses such a section outright as
corrupt:

    ld: error: BSS.OBJ(BUFFER) is too large (0x7d0 bytes)
    ld: final link failed: file truncated

That is a MACRO-11 module with a 1000-word array in it, assembled by
RT-11's own MACRO, and it could not be linked at all -- the array is
2000 bytes and the whole object file is 512.

So note whether any text landed in a p-sect, and for one where none
did, drop SEC_HAS_CONTENTS and SEC_LOAD once the module has been read.
What was already right stays right: a p-sect with text and a hole in
the middle (the location counter steps over it, RLD entry type 010)
keeps its contents, hole and all, since the hole is zeros that belong
in the image.

The same module now links, and the array costs nothing in the file:
BUFFER lands after .bss as a section with an address and no contents,
the image is 1024 bytes rather than 3072, and word 050 of the header
covers the array exactly (005546 = the end of BUFFER).  Reading DEC's
SYSLIB.OBJ is unaffected -- its 112 modules have no such p-sect -- and
every example and test still builds and passes on the machine.
---
 bfd/obj-pdp11.c | 20 ++++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 20 insertions(+)

diff -urN a/bfd/obj-pdp11.c b/bfd/obj-pdp11.c
--- a/bfd/obj-pdp11.c	2026-10-10 19:30:00
+++ b/bfd/obj-pdp11.c	2026-10-10 19:30:00
@@ -185,6 +185,7 @@
   bfd_byte *contents;
   arelent *relocs;
   unsigned int relcount;	/* Counted, then filled.  */
+  bool had_text;		/* Some TXT record landed in this p-sect.  */
   bool unsupported;		/* Carries an RLD entry we cannot use.  */
 };
 
@@ -825,6 +826,9 @@
     {
       bfd_vma where = bfd_getl16 (body);
       struct obj_pdp11_section_data *data = obj_section_data (walk->current);
+
+      if (data != NULL && size > 2)
+	data->had_text = true;
       bfd_byte *contents = data != NULL ? data->contents : NULL;
       size_t n = size - 2;
 
@@ -945,7 +949,23 @@
   walk.current = NULL;
   walk.counting = false;
   obj_pdp11_walk_records (&walk, buf, size);
+
+  /* A p-sect no TXT record ever covered is length without content --
+     what .BLKW writes, and what a C compiler would have called .bss.
+     Saying it has contents would be a lie with teeth: the contents are
+     bigger than the file they supposedly come from, and
+     bfd_get_full_section_contents refuses such a section outright as
+     corrupt ("is too large"), which is a module that cannot be linked
+     at all.  */
+  for (sec = abfd->sections; sec != NULL; sec = sec->next)
+    {
+      struct obj_pdp11_section_data *data = obj_section_data (sec);
 
+      if (data == NULL || data->had_text || sec->size == 0)
+	continue;
+      sec->flags &= ~(SEC_HAS_CONTENTS | SEC_LOAD);
+    }
+
   free (buf);
 
   abfd->flags |= HAS_SYMS | HAS_RELOC;
